# What never belongs in the build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the important one. Speed: the context is
# uploaded to the daemon before anything runs, and bin/obj across four projects
# is hundreds of megabytes. Safety: .env holds every live credential this
# project has, and a build context is trivially readable out of any layer it
# lands in — `COPY . .` would put it in the image whether or not the Dockerfile
# ever mentions it.

# Secrets. Never, under any pattern.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pfx
*.p12
**/appsettings.*.local.json

# Build output — restored and rebuilt inside the image.
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/.vs/
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/

# Local data that happens to sit in the project folder.
**/uploads/
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Tests: the image never runs them, and PL is referenced transitively so
# including the project would drag the whole xunit graph into a runtime image.
Life-Admin-Autopilot.Tests/

# Repo furniture with no bearing on the build.
.git/
.github/
.claude/
docs/
langflow/
tools/
*.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
